<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porfirio Díaz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porfirio Díaz was a Mexican general, hero of the war against the French intervention, who seized power in 1876 and ruled almost continuously until 1911. The period, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfiriato">Porfiriato</a>, brought railways, mines and foreign investment but also great concentration of land and repression. His fraudulent re-election in 1910 sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz.jpg" alt="Porfirio Díaz" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aurelio Escobar Castellanos, Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porfirio_diaz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Porfirio Díaz</b> foreign capital, much of it American and British, built a railway network of some 19,000 kilometres by 1910, linking mines and plantations to the United States border and the ports, and Mexico became a major producer of silver, copper, henequen and, from the 1900s, oil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Latin America the wars of independence were followed by the rule of caudillos, such as José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia, perpetual dictator of Paraguay from 1816, and <b>Porfirio Díaz</b>, who governed Mexico for most of the years from 1876 to 1911.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Many presidents ruled as caudillos, extended their own terms or were removed by revolts and coups; Mexico’s revolution against the three-decade rule of <b>Porfirio Díaz</b> produced the constitution of 1917, which forbids any president a second term.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldiers of <b>Porfirio Díaz</b>'s army sang of a soldadera who wanted money for the bullfights; for Villa's men, as the historian Elizabeth Salas writes, the cockroach wanted money for alcohol and marijuana.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oaxaca de Juárez, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.06,-96.73&amp;year=1885" aria-label="Oaxaca de Juárez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1920 1530 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Porfirio Díaz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2370 1800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2370 1800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.06,-96.73&amp;year=1885">What was here in 1885? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
